A relation is an equivalence relation if it is reflexive, symmetric and transitive.

**Reflexive.** Every element is related to itself: $(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (4,4)$ are all in $R$. So $R$ is reflexive.

**Symmetric.** For each pair $(a, b)\in R$ the pair $(b, a)$ must also be in $R$. The pairs $(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (4,4)$ are their own reverse. The pair $(1,3)\in R$ has reverse $(3,1)\in R$, and $(3,1)\in R$ has reverse $(1,3)\in R$. So $R$ is symmetric.

**Transitive.** If $(a,b)\in R$ and $(b,c)\in R$ then $(a,c)$ must be in $R$. Only the elements $1$ and $3$ are related to anything other than themselves, so the non-trivial chains are:

- $(1,3), (3,1) \Rightarrow (1,1)\in R$;
- $(3,1), (1,3) \Rightarrow (3,3)\in R$;
- $(1,3), (3,3) \Rightarrow (1,3)\in R$;
- $(3,1), (1,1) \Rightarrow (3,1)\in R$;
- $(1,1), (1,3) \Rightarrow (1,3)\in R$;
- $(3,3), (3,1) \Rightarrow (3,1)\in R$.

Chains through $2$ or $4$ only use $(2,2)$ or $(4,4)$ and give the same pair back. So $R$ is transitive.

Since $R$ is reflexive, symmetric and transitive, $R$ is an equivalence relation on $S$. $\blacksquare$
